# Infrastructure (Kubernetes + Helm)

## Structure

- `infra/k8s/base/` holds environment-agnostic manifests; `infra/k8s/overlays/{dev,prod}/` holds Kustomize patches.
- Helm charts live in `infra/helm/<chart>/`. Every environment value belongs in `values-{env}.yaml`, never inside a template default that hides prod behavior.
- One workload per directory, named after the service (`web` for Next.js, `api` for Spring Boot).

## Required on every workload

Reject a manifest that is missing any of these:

- `resources.requests` and `resources.limits` for CPU and memory.
- `readinessProbe` and `livenessProbe` on a real endpoint — Spring Boot uses `/actuator/health/readiness` and `/actuator/health/liveness`; Next.js needs a dedicated lightweight route.
- Immutable image reference: a digest or a release tag. `:latest` is never acceptable.
- `securityContext` with `runAsNonRoot: true`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` where feasible.
- Labels `app.kubernetes.io/name`, `app.kubernetes.io/component`, `app.kubernetes.io/version`.
- `RollingUpdate` strategy with `maxUnavailable: 0` for user-facing services, plus a `PodDisruptionBudget`.

## Configuration and secrets

- Non-sensitive config in a `ConfigMap`, consumed as env vars via `envFrom`.
- Secrets come from a `Secret` populated by an external store (External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, cloud secret manager). Never a literal value or a base64 blob in git.
- Database credentials are injected as env vars matching the Spring Boot property names; the connection string never appears in a manifest.

## Frontend/backend split

- Next.js and Spring Boot are separate Deployments and Services, scaled independently.
- The browser reaches the API through Ingress path routing or a Next.js Route Handler — the Spring Boot Service is not exposed publicly unless the spec says so.
- Server-side Next.js calls the backend via the in-cluster DNS name (`http://api.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local:8080`).

## Validation before anything else

```bash
kustomize build infra/k8s/overlays/<env> | kubeconform -strict -summary -
helm lint infra/helm/<chart>
helm template <release> infra/helm/<chart> -f infra/helm/<chart>/values-<env>.yaml | kubeconform -strict -
```

## Cluster operations

- Read-only inspection through the `kubernetes` MCP server (get, describe, logs, events) is always allowed and is the preferred way to diagnose a failing rollout.
- Mutating operations — `apply`, `delete`, `scale`, `rollout restart`, `exec` — require explicit user approval per action. Propose the exact command and wait.
- Never touch a production context without the user naming it. Confirm the current context before any read that could be misread as prod.
- After an approved rollout, verify with `rollout status`, then pod events and logs; report the real output.
